.TH ZMTX 1 "August 24, 2026"
.SH NAME
zmtx \- transmit file using ZMODEM protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zmtx
[
.B \-48snopdv
] [
.B \-l\fIline\fP
] [
.B \-w\fIbytes\fP
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I zmtx
transmits a file using the ZMODEM file transfer protocol.
By default,
.I zmtx
reads
.I stdin
and writes
.IR stdout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-4
Use ZedZap data subpackets up to 4 KiB.  The sender starts with a
standard 1 KiB subpacket and increases the size after clean transfers.
.TP
.B \-8
Use ZedZap data subpackets up to 8 KiB.  This extension is not negotiated;
use it only with a receiver known to accept ZedZap subpackets.
.TP
.B \-s
Disable streaming and wait for a committed-position acknowledgement after
every data subpacket.  This mode is also selected automatically when the
receiver does not advertise overlapped I/O capability.
.TP
.B \-w\fIbytes\fP
Limit the amount of transmitted but unacknowledged file data.  The value is
in bytes and may have a case-insensitive binary K or M suffix.  The window
must hold at least four maximum-size data subpackets, so the minimum is 4 KiB
normally, 16 KiB with
.BR \-4 ,
and 32 KiB with
.BR \-8 .
The sender requests acknowledgements at approximately quarter-window
intervals.  If the receiver is not full duplex, the sender safely falls back
to waiting after every block.  This option cannot be combined with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-l\fIline\fP
File (typically a tty) to use for the transfer instead
of
.IR stdin/stdout.
.TP
.B \-n
Transfer only if source is newer than destination.
.TP
.B \-o,\-p
Overwrite (protect) the destination if it exists.
The default is to protect the destination.  These
flags interact in a complicated way with similar
options at the receiving end -- see the protocol
specification, or be careful.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode.
.TP
.B \-d
Debug mode.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PD
.BR  zmrx(1), sz(1), rz(1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jacques Mattheij <jacquesm@hacktic.nl>
